Here is the most common and modern way to center a `div` both horizontally and vertically using **Flexbox**:

```css
.parent-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Centers horizontally */
  align-items: center;     /* Centers vertically */
  height: 100vh;           /* Optional: Makes the parent fill the full viewport */
}

.child-div {
  /* Your child div styles go here */
}
```

**How it works:**
1.  **`display: flex;`**: Turns the parent into a flexible container.
2.  **`justify-content: center;`**: Aligns items along the main axis (usually horizontal).
3.  **`align-items: center;`**: Aligns items along the cross axis (usually vertical).
4.  **`height: 100vh;`**: Ensures the parent has a defined height so vertical centering works. If your parent already has a fixed height, you can remove this line.

### Alternative: Grid (Modern & Concise)
If you prefer CSS Grid, this achieves the same result in fewer lines:

```css
.parent-container {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */
  height: 100vh;
}

.child-div {
  /* Your child div styles go here */
}
```
